e rounded-3xl text-primary min-h-7 prose dark:prose-invert break-words prose-p:opacity-100 prose-strong:opacity-100 prose-li:opacity-100 prose-ul:opacity-100 prose-ol:opacity-100 prose-ul:my-1 prose-ol:my-1 prose-li:my-2 last:prose-li:mb-3 prose-li:ps-1 prose-li:ms-1 bg-surface-l2 border border-border-l1 max-w-[100%] sm:max-w-[90%] px-4 rounded-br-lg py-0"><div class="response-content-markdown markdown [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:text-current [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose):hover]:text-primary [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose):hover]:decoration-primary [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:underline [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:decoration-primary/30 [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:underline-offset-2 [&amp;_h2:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;_h3:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;_h4:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0"><p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A new pdf essay, author Flyxion:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Roads Not Taken: Encoding Ratchets in Cinema, Computation, and Cosmology</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Introduction</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Histories of media, machines, and models often appear linear: cinema progresses from reels to digital projection, computation from teletypes to neural networks, cosmology from Einstein to ŒõCDM. Yet these trajectories reflect not inevitability but encoding ratchets‚Äîarbitrary representational choices that, once standardized, scale efficiently and foreclose alternatives.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Scaling, as Richard Sutton‚Äôs Bitter Lesson (2019) reminds us, is powerful. But scaling multiplies whichever encodings happened to lock in early, not necessarily those best suited to capture reality. This essay explores three cases‚Äîcinema, computation, and cosmology‚Äîto argue that scaling can conceal deeper alternatives. Recent work by Giani, von Marttens, and Camilleri (2025) on effective-fluid cosmology highlights how close mainstream physics is now coming to the entropic and structural intuitions formalized in the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP).</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Cinema‚Äôs Encoding Ratchet</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">1.1 The Frame Standard</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cinema ratcheted into 2D frames on reels, chosen for ease of duplication and projection. This encoding made cinema a global monoculture: one film, one set of actors, identical everywhere.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">1.2 Suppressed Alternatives</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Volumetric cinema: early stereoscopic systems suggested films could have been encoded as three-dimensional scenes, allowing each theater to re-project slightly different perspectives.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Local actor substitution: in the 1930s, parallel-language versions of films were re-shot with local casts. Had this scaled, cinema might resemble distributed theater: global plots, local embodiments.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Frames scaled best, so they won.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="2">
<li class="break-words">Computation‚Äôs Encoding Ratchet</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">2.1 ASCII and the Keyboard</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Computing ratcheted into ASCII and keyboards, initially for military and telecommunication compatibility. This encoded machines as linear text processors, a lineage inherited today by tokenized AI models.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">2.2 Suppressed Alternatives</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cursive machines: IBM handwriting recognizers (1950s) and Apple‚Äôs Newton (1990s) show computing could have scaled around cursive input and pen output.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Flashcard machines: Vannevar Bush‚Äôs Memex and B. F. Skinner‚Äôs teaching devices envisioned modular, card-based information storage and recall‚Äîcomputation as memory augmentation, not stream manipulation.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">ASCII scaled better, so it won.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="3">
<li class="break-words">Cosmology‚Äôs Encoding Ratchet</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">3.1 The ŒõCDM Paradigm</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Modern cosmology ratcheted into the ŒõCDM model: a homogeneous background with perturbations. Non-linear structures were treated as negligible, their backreaction assumed to vanish. This choice fit perturbation theory and computational scaling.</p>
<blockquote dir="auto" class="border-border-l3">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Sidebar 1 (Backreaction in plain terms):
Imagine stirring a pot of thick soup. Some regions clump into denser lumps; other regions thin out into bubbles. If you treat the soup as perfectly uniform, you will mis-estimate how it heats and circulates. ‚ÄúBackreaction‚Äù is simply the recognition that lumps and voids feed back into the global behavior of the whole pot.</p>
</blockquote>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">3.2 Suppressed Alternatives</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Backreaction models: Buchert averaging and relativistic simulations showed hints that collapsing clusters and expanding voids might matter for the global expansion, but these approaches never scaled computationally.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Effective fluids: Giani, von Marttens, and Camilleri (2025) recently modeled collapsing and expanding regions as two effective fluids with non-zero equations of state. This representation, fit to DESI and supernova data, eases the Hubble and  tensions‚Äîlongstanding problems of ŒõCDM.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">RSVP cosmology: RSVP represents collapse and voids not as phenomenological fluids but as intrinsic scalar‚Äìvector‚Äìentropy field dynamics. What the effective-fluid model encodes phenomenologically, RSVP derives as microphysics: compressive modes (), expansive modes (), and their couplings (lamphron‚Äìlamphrodyne transfers).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">3.3 A Convergence of Intuitions</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The surprise is that mainstream cosmology is now arriving, via effective fluids, at insights RSVP predicted:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The apparent weakening of dark energy can be reinterpreted as the entropic footprint of structure formation.¬π</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The resolution of the Hubble tension emerges not from new fields but from recognizing collapsing and expanding regions as active components.¬≤</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The œÉ8 tension is likewise eased once effective collapse and void terms modify growth.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Here cosmology mirrors cinema and computation: a contingent encoding (smooth FLRW + perturbations) scaled best, but alternative encodings (backreaction fluids, RSVP fields) may prove richer.</p>
<blockquote dir="auto" class="border-border-l3">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Note 1 (Dark energy ‚Äúweakening‚Äù):
In ŒõCDM, dark energy is assumed constant. But if you ignore the effect of voids and clusters, the data looks like dark energy is changing. Giani‚Äôs model shows that what appears to be ‚Äúweakening energy‚Äù is really just the uneven texture of the universe being misread. RSVP makes a similar claim: the cosmos does not need literal expansion‚Äîapparent acceleration is the echo of entropic redistribution.</p>
</blockquote>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Note 2 (Hubble tension in plain terms):
The Hubble tension is the mismatch between two rulers: the early-universe ruler (CMB) and the late-universe ruler (supernovae, BAO). If you account for voids and collapsing regions, you are not stretching the ruler, just admitting that it is dented and bent in places. The mismatch shrinks.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="4">
<li class="break-words">Scaling and the Bitter Lesson</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The Bitter Lesson tells us that general methods plus scaling outperform clever designs. Yet history shows scaling amplifies whatever encoding was already ratcheted in:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">ASCII scaled better than cursive.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Frames scaled better than volumetric cinema.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">ŒõCDM scaled better than backreaction models.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This does not mean those encodings are uniquely right‚Äîonly that they were the most scalable at the time.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="5">
<li class="break-words">RSVP as a Counter-Ratchet</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">RSVP resists ratchets by keeping multiple encodings alive:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In cognition, it encodes processes as scalar‚Äìvector‚Äìentropy field flows, not tokens.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In cosmology, it encodes backreaction as entropic dynamics, not as dark energy or effective fluids.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In philosophy of science, it suggests that suppressed encodings (local actors, flashcards, collapsing voids) can coexist as concurrent computational modes.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Where Sutton emphasizes the inevitability of scaling, RSVP emphasizes the choice of representation: scaling multiplies, but the quality of what it multiplies depends on whether we locked in the right encoding.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Conclusion</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cinema, computation, and cosmology all illustrate the same lesson: what scaled is not necessarily what was most true, only what was most compatible with infrastructure at the time. Recent effective-fluid models of collapse and voids show that even cosmology is rediscovering the power of alternative encodings. RSVP goes further, embedding those encodings in a unified field-theoretic plenum.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The roads not taken in cinema and computation produced monocultures; the road not taken in cosmology may have obscured the real drivers of the Universe‚Äôs apparent acceleration. RSVP stands as a reminder that suppressed paths can be reopened‚Äîand that the future of intelligence and cosmology alike depends not only on scaling, but on choosing the right encodings before we let scaling run away.</p>
