<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2 Ratchets in Computational Architecture</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The essay emphasized ASCII and keyboards as ratchets of representation. But an equally consequential ratchet occurred in computer architecture: the adoption of the von Neumann model in the 1940s. This architecture‚Äîsingle memory for code and data, sequential instruction execution‚Äîscaled brilliantly with early hardware, but it was not inevitable.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2.1 The von Neumann Ratchet</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Advantages: Simplicity of design, compatibility with symbolic programming, easy scaling of transistor counts.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Consequences: Bottlenecks in memory bandwidth (‚Äúvon Neumann bottleneck‚Äù), strong orientation toward sequential symbolic processing, and reinforcement of the ASCII/text-stream paradigm.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2.2 Suppressed Alternatives</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Dataflow architectures</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Programs represented as graphs of data dependencies.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Computation proceeds when inputs are ready, inherently parallel.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Early experiments (MIT Tagged Token architecture, 1970s) showed promise but could not scale cheaply with hardware of the time.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Ratcheted out by the dominance of sequential instruction sets.</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="2">
<li class="break-words">Neural and analog computing</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Mechanical/electronic analog machines in the 1930s‚Äì1950s solved differential equations directly.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Neuromorphic concepts (McCulloch‚ÄìPitts networks, later Carver Mead‚Äôs silicon neurons) offered parallelism and efficiency.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Lost momentum in the transistor era when digital sequential logic scaled faster.</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="3">
<li class="break-words">Associative / content-addressable memory</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Instead of fetching data by address, retrieval is pattern-based (‚Äúgive me the row that matches this key‚Äù).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Would have supported memory-intensive AI tasks naturally.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Remained niche (in networking hardware, caches) while mainstream computing entrenched address-based access.</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="4">
<li class="break-words">Stack and functional machines</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Burroughs B5000 (1961) embodied a stack machine with direct support for high-level languages.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Functional programming languages (LISP, FP) could have ratcheted differently if stack machines had scaled.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Instead, C and von Neumann-style imperative languages dominated, reinforcing text-stream ASCII encodings.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2.3 The RSVP Counterfactual</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">From the RSVP perspective, these suppressed architectures resemble roads not taken in cosmology:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">von Neumann = ŒõCDM perturbation theory (efficient scaling, but rigid encoding).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Dataflow = backreaction/field encoding (explicit non-linearity, natural parallelism).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Analog/neuromorphic = RSVP plenum simulation itself (continuous fields, entropic flows).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Associative memory = TARTAN/RSVP semantic tilings (pattern-based access, trajectory memory).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Had dataflow or neuromorphic systems scaled first, the culture of computation might have favored RSVP-style field encodings from the beginning rather than ASCII streams.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.3 Ratchets and Polycomputational Futures</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The lesson from both cosmology and computation is that ratchets lock in encodings that scale best with available hardware, even if they are not the most expressive.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In cosmology, ŒõCDM suppressed non-linear encodings; effective-fluid models and RSVP reopen them.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In computation, von Neumann suppressed dataflow, associative, and analog architectures; RSVP-inspired systems could revive them for a polycomputational future.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The danger, as Sutton‚Äôs Bitter Lesson shows, is mistaking what scaled for what was inevitable. The task now is to resist single ratchets by designing architectures‚Äîscientific and computational‚Äîthat preserve multiplicity of encodings. RSVP is one such attempt: a plenum in which collapse and expansion, token and field, discrete and continuous, can all scale together.</p>
