<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Appendix: Alternative Encodings in Cosmology and Computation</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.1 Effective Fluids and RSVP Field Dynamics</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The effective-fluid model of Giani, von Marttens, and Camilleri (2025) re-encodes non-linear inhomogeneities as two interacting fluids: collapsing overdense regions () and expanding voids (). RSVP provides a natural microphysical foundation:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Collapse = regions where , negentropic compression.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Void = regions where , entropic expansion.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Couplings  = lamphron‚Äìlamphrodyne transfers of energy between dust, compression, and expansion.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Thus, what the effective-fluid model represents phenomenologically, RSVP produces dynamically. The fact that mainstream cosmology has rediscovered these terms is evidence that RSVP‚Äôs encoding captures genuine physical structure.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2 Ratchets in Computational Architecture</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The adoption of the von Neumann architecture‚Äîa sequential model where a single memory stores both instructions and data‚Äîwas not inevitable. It scaled well with vacuum tubes and later transistors, but other architectures existed that might have locked in had conditions been slightly different.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2.1 Dataflow Machines</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Concept: Programs represented as dependency graphs. Computation proceeds when inputs are ready; instructions fire asynchronously.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Examples: MIT Tagged Token Dataflow Machine (1970s), Manchester Dataflow Computer (1980s).</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Promise: Natural parallelism; eliminates the von Neumann bottleneck.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Failure to scale: Hardware complexity and lack of compiler support made them slower and more expensive than von Neumann machines in the short term.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Counterfactual: If dataflow had scaled, programming would look more like wiring RSVP‚Äôs field equations directly‚Äîflows, dependencies, and event-driven computation‚Äîrather than issuing sequential instructions.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2.2 Analog and Neuromorphic Computing</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Analog machines: Vannevar Bush‚Äôs Differential Analyzer (1930s) solved differential equations with gears, shafts, and wheels.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Neuromorphic chips: Carver Mead (1980s) pioneered silicon neurons and synapses, aiming for brainlike computation.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Promise: Direct representation of continuous dynamics; energy-efficient and massively parallel.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Failure to scale: Digital logic was easier to miniaturize and manufacture reliably.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Counterfactual: An analog/neuromorphic ratchet would have normalized RSVP-style continuous field dynamics, making scalar‚Äìvector‚Äìentropy simulation the default mode of computing.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2.3 Associative and Content-Addressable Memory (CAM)</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Concept: Retrieve data by its content, not by an address. Ask the memory, ‚ÄúWho matches this pattern?‚Äù rather than ‚ÄúWhat is stored at address X?‚Äù</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Examples: Early associative memories in the 1960s; used in networking and caches but never mainstream.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Promise: Pattern recognition and search become fundamental operations‚Äîideal for AI.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Failure to scale: Too costly in silicon area; industry standardized on address-based RAM.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Counterfactual: An associative-memory ratchet would have fit perfectly with RSVP‚Äôs TARTAN framework: computation as semantic tiling and trajectory memory, not instruction‚Äìaddress cycles.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.2.4 Stack and Functional Machines</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Burroughs B5000 (1961): A pioneering stack machine designed to run ALGOL directly. Programs executed as high-level blocks; hardware understood recursion.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Promise: Close alignment between human reasoning (functions, recursion) and machine execution.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Failure to scale: Less efficient for low-level tasks, overtaken by IBM System/360 and C-based architectures.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Counterfactual: If the B5000 lineage had won, programming culture might have ratcheted toward functional abstraction and symbolic manipulation, not imperative token streams. RSVP‚Äôs categorical formulations would feel less alien in such an ecosystem.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.3 RSVP and Architectural Counterfactuals</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Seen through RSVP, each alternative architecture corresponds to a ‚Äúroad not taken‚Äù in computation, much like effective-fluid cosmology represents a road not taken in physics:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Von Neumann ‚Üî ŒõCDM perturbation theory: Scales well, but encodes reality narrowly.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Dataflow ‚Üî backreaction averaging: Emphasizes flows and dependencies, natural parallelism, but too costly early on.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Analog/neuromorphic ‚Üî RSVP plenum: Continuous fields, entropic flows, microphysical realism; suppressed by digital ratchet.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Associative memory ‚Üî TARTAN/semantic tilings: Pattern-based retrieval and modular computation; eclipsed by address-based memory.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Stack machines ‚Üî categorical/sheaf encodings: Function-first design, structurally aligned with higher mathematics; suppressed by imperative hardware/software co-evolution.</p>
<hr class="border-border-l2">
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">A.4 Polycomputational Futures</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The common lesson across cinema, computation, and cosmology is that ratchets lock in whichever encoding scaled first, suppressing equally valid alternatives. RSVP is explicitly designed to resist such ratchets by enabling polycomputation: multiple encodings (field, token, associative, functional) scaling together, rather than one monopolizing all resources.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In cosmology, this means entropic backreaction alongside ŒõCDM-like averages.
In computation, this means associative, neuromorphic, and dataflow components running in concert with sequential processors.
In culture, this means volumetric cinema alongside frame reels, or local actor substitutions alongside global blockbusters.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Scaling is not the enemy; it is the multiplier. But only if we keep the encodings plural.</p>
