cs, and (iv) cleanly exposes where ‚Äúentropic‚Äù effects enter as effective pressure/backreaction.
0) Variables and frames</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Background FLRW scale factor a(t)a(t), Hubble H=aÀô/aH=\dot a/a. Physical position r=a(t)‚Äâx \mathbf{r}=a(t),\mathbf{x} with comoving x\mathbf{x}.</li>
<li class="break-words">Mass‚Äìenergy density œÅ(x,t) \rho(\mathbf{x},t) (you can call this Œ¶ \Phi if you prefer).</li>
<li class="break-words">Peculiar velocity (in comoving coordinates) v(x,t)=xÀô \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x},t)=\dot{\mathbf{x}} so the physical velocity is u=Hr+a‚Äâv \mathbf{u}=H\mathbf{r}+a,\mathbf{v}.</li>
<li class="break-words">‚ÄúEntropy field‚Äù s(x,t) s(\mathbf{x},t) (entropy density per comoving volume).</li>
<li class="break-words">Temperature proxy T(x,t)T(\mathbf{x},t) (only used to enforce non-negative production; you can set T‚ÜíT‚ãÜT\to T_\star constant if you prefer a purely kinematic closure).</li>
<li class="break-words">Expansion scalar Œ∏‚â°3H+‚àá‚Äâ‚Å£‚ãÖ‚Äâ‚Å£v \theta \equiv 3H + \nabla!\cdot!\mathbf{v} (comoving divergence), shear tensor œÉij\sigma_{ij}.</li>
</ul>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Local balance laws (minimal form)
Work in Newtonian gauge on sub-horizon scales or as an effective fluid in GR; the forms below are identical either way.
(M) Continuity
‚àÇtœÅ+3HœÅ+‚àá‚Äâ‚Å£‚ãÖ(œÅ‚Äâv)‚ÄÖ‚Ää=‚ÄÖ‚Ää0.\partial_t \rho + 3H\rho + \nabla!\cdot(\rho,\mathbf{v}) ;=; 0.
(P) Momentum (Euler with entropic stress)
‚àÇtv+(v‚Äâ‚Å£‚ãÖ‚Äâ‚Å£‚àá)v+H‚Äâv=‚àí‚Äâ1a2‚Äâ‚àáŒ¶N‚ÄÖ‚Ää‚àí‚ÄÖ‚Ää1a2(œÅc2)‚Äâ‚àá‚Äâ‚Å£(p+Œ†S)‚ÄÖ‚Ää+‚ÄÖ‚Ää1a2œÅc2‚Äâ‚àá‚Äâ‚Å£‚ãÖœÄ,\partial_t \mathbf{v} + (\mathbf{v}!\cdot!\nabla)\mathbf{v} + H,\mathbf{v} = -,\frac{1}{a^2},\nabla \Phi_N ;-; \frac{1}{a^2(\rho c^2)},\nabla!\big(p + \Pi_S\big) ;+; \frac{1}{a^2\rho c^2},\nabla!\cdot \boldsymbol{\pi},
where Œ¶N\Phi_N is the Newtonian gravitational potential (or use GR below), pp is the thermodynamic pressure of the material content, Œ†S\Pi_S is an entropic (bulk-like) pressure, and œÄ\boldsymbol{\pi} is an (optional) traceless anisotropic stress (shear).
(G) Gravity (choose one)
‚Ä¢ Newtonian: ‚àá2Œ¶N=4œÄGa2(œÅ‚àíœÅÀâ)\nabla^2 \Phi_N = 4\pi G a^2(\rho - \bar\rho).
‚Ä¢ Fully relativistic: use TŒºŒΩT^{\mu\nu} below inside Einstein‚Äôs field equations.
(S) Entropy balance (second law)
‚àÇts+3Hs+‚àá‚Äâ‚Å£‚ãÖ‚Äâ‚Å£(s‚Äâv‚àíDs‚àás)‚ÄÖ‚Ää=‚ÄÖ‚ÄäŒ£(x,t),Œ£‚â•0.\partial_t s + 3H s + \nabla!\cdot!\big(s,\mathbf{v} - D_s \nabla s\big) ;=; \Sigma(\mathbf{x},t), \qquad \Sigma \ge 0.</li>
<li class="break-words">Minimal constitutive closure (guarantees Œ£‚â•0\Sigma\ge 0)
Introduce the two couplings that make RSVP ‚Äúentropic‚Äù without new exotic fields:
(C1) Entropic bulk pressure (void/filament driver)
Œ†S=‚àí‚ÄâŒ∂s‚ÄâŒ∏‚ÄÖ‚Ää+‚ÄÖ‚Ääœás‚Äâ‚àá2s.\Pi_S = -,\zeta_s,\theta ;+; \chi_s,\nabla^2 s.</li>
</ol>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Œ∂s‚â•0 \zeta_s \ge 0 (bulk-viscous‚Äìlike coefficient tied to local expansion/compression); ‚àíŒ∂sŒ∏-\zeta_s\theta gives negative pressure in expanding regions (voids) and positive in collapsing regions (overdensities).</li>
<li class="break-words">œás‚â•0 \chi_s \ge 0 couples entropy gradients to pressure (entropy-rich regions push outward).</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">(C2) Entropy production density
Œ£‚ÄÖ‚Ää=‚ÄÖ‚ÄäŒ∂sT‚ÄâŒ∏2‚ÄÖ‚Ää+‚ÄÖ‚ÄäœásT‚Äâ‚à£‚àá2s‚à£2‚ÄÖ‚Ää+‚ÄÖ‚ÄäŒ∑sT‚ÄâœÉijœÉij‚ÄÖ‚Ää‚ÄÖ‚Ää(‚â•0),\Sigma ;=; \frac{\zeta_s}{T},\theta^2 ;+; \frac{\chi_s}{T},|\nabla^2 s|^2 ;+; \frac{\eta_s}{T},\sigma_{ij}\sigma^{ij} ;;(\ge 0),
with an optional shear term Œ∑s‚Äâ‚Å£‚â•‚Äâ‚Å£0\eta_s!\ge!0. This is the standard Onsager-type quadratic form; it ensures the second law locally without specifying microphysics.
(Remark: If you want the leanest possible model, set Œ∑s=0\eta_s=0 and keep Œ∂s,œás\zeta_s,\chi_s only.)
3) Relativistic packaging (if you prefer GR throughout)
Define an effective imperfect fluid:
TŒºŒΩ=(œÅc2+p+Œ†S)‚ÄâuŒºuŒΩ+(p+Œ†S)‚ÄâgŒºŒΩ+œÄŒºŒΩ,T^{\mu\nu}=(\rho c^2 + p + \Pi_S),u^\mu u^\nu + (p + \Pi_S),g^{\mu\nu} + \pi^{\mu\nu},
with uŒºu^\mu the 4-velocity (uŒºuŒº=‚àí1u^\mu u_\mu=-1), œÄŒºŒΩ\pi^{\mu\nu} traceless and orthogonal to uŒºu^\mu. Then Einstein:
GŒºŒΩ=8œÄGc4‚ÄâTŒºŒΩ.G^{\mu\nu}=\frac{8\pi G}{c^4},T^{\mu\nu}.
The entropy current sŒº=suŒº‚àíDs‚àáŒºss^\mu = s u^\mu - D_s \nabla^\mu s satisfies ‚àáŒºsŒº=Œ£‚â•0\nabla_\mu s^\mu=\Sigma\ge 0.
This is a one-line way to insert RSVP‚Äôs entropic effects into GR while preserving covariance.
4) Coarse-graining to a Friedmann-like system
Average over a compact comoving domain D\mathcal{D} with volume VD(t)V_\mathcal{D}(t) and domain scale factor
aD(t)‚ÄÖ‚Ää‚â°‚ÄÖ‚Ää(VD(t)VD(t0))1/3,HD‚â°aÀôD/aD.a_\mathcal{D}(t) ;\equiv; \Big(\frac{V_\mathcal{D}(t)}{V_\mathcal{D}(t_0)}\Big)^{1/3}, \qquad H_\mathcal{D} \equiv \dot a_\mathcal{D}/a_\mathcal{D}.
Denote spatial averages by ‚ü®‚ãÖ‚ü©D\langle \cdot \rangle_\mathcal{D}. Then the averaged (Buchert-style) equations with RSVP terms are:
(F1) Hamiltonian constraint
3HD‚Äâ2‚ÄÖ‚Ää=‚ÄÖ‚Ää8œÄGc2‚Äâ‚ü®œÅ‚ü©D‚ÄÖ‚Ää‚àí‚ÄÖ‚Ää12‚Äâ‚ü®R‚ü©D‚ÄÖ‚Ää‚àí‚ÄÖ‚Ää12‚ÄâQkin‚ÄÖ‚Ää+‚ÄÖ‚Ää8œÄGc4‚Äâ‚ü®Œ†S‚ü©D‚èüRSVP&nbsp;entropic&nbsp;term,3 H_\mathcal{D}^{,2} ;=; \frac{8\pi G}{c^2},\langle \rho \rangle_\mathcal{D} ;-; \tfrac{1}{2},\langle \mathcal{R} \rangle_\mathcal{D} ;-; \tfrac{1}{2},Q_{\rm kin} ;+; \underbrace{\frac{8\pi G}{c^4},\langle \Pi_S \rangle_\mathcal{D}}<em>{\text{RSVP entropic term}},
(F2) Raychaudhuri (acceleration)
3‚Äâa¬®DaD‚ÄÖ‚Ää=‚ÄÖ‚Ää‚àí4œÄGc2(‚ü®œÅc2+3(p+Œ†S)‚ü©D)‚ÄÖ‚Ää+‚ÄÖ‚ÄäQkin,3,\frac{\ddot a</em>\mathcal{D}}{a_\mathcal{D}} ;=; -\frac{4\pi G}{c^2}\Big(\langle \rho c^2 + 3(p+\Pi_S) \rangle_\mathcal{D}\Big) ;+; Q_{\rm kin},
(F3) Matter conservation
‚àÇt‚ü®œÅ‚ü©D+3HD‚Äâ‚ü®œÅ‚ü©D‚ÄÖ‚Ää=‚ÄÖ‚Ää0,\partial_t \langle \rho \rangle_\mathcal{D} + 3 H_\mathcal{D},\langle \rho \rangle_\mathcal{D} ;=; 0,
(F4) Integrability (curvature‚Äìbackreaction coupling)
‚àÇt‚Äâ‚Å£(aD6Qkin)‚ÄÖ‚Ää+‚ÄÖ‚Ääc2aD4‚Äâ‚àÇt‚Äâ‚Å£(aD2‚ü®R‚ü©D)‚ÄÖ‚Ää=‚ÄÖ‚Ää0.\partial_t!\Big(a_\mathcal{D}^{6} Q_{\rm kin}\Big) ;+; c^2 a_\mathcal{D}^{4},\partial_t!\Big(a_\mathcal{D}^2 \langle \mathcal{R} \rangle_\mathcal{D}\Big) ;=; 0.
Here
(\displaystyle Q_{\rm kin} \equiv \frac{2}{3}\big\langle(\theta - \langle\theta\rangle_\mathcal{D})^2\big\rangle_\mathcal{D}</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">2\langle \sigma_{ij}\sigma^{ij}\rangle_\mathcal{D}) (kinematic backreaction from inhomogeneities), and R\mathcal{R} is the 3-Ricci scalar of the spatial slices.
&nbsp;&nbsp;RSVP enters only through Œ†S\Pi_S (and, if you keep œás\chi_s, through small corrections to QkinQ_{\rm kin} via œÉ\sigma and Œ∏\theta).</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Key point: In a strictly homogeneous/isotropic limit (v=0\mathbf{v}=0, ‚àás=0\nabla s=0, Qkin=0Q_{\rm kin}=0, ‚ü®R‚ü©D=6k/a2\langle \mathcal{R} \rangle_\mathcal{D} = 6k/a^2), the system reduces to Friedmann with an effective pressure
